on this first day of february  journalists in mexico are mourning the  murder of yet another colleague the  fourth to be killed since the start of  this year mexico city ranks as one of  the most dangerous places in the world  for journalists reporters are being  targeted for exposing corruption at the  top the murders have sparked angry  protests and the government is under  pressure to make it safer for  journalists to do their job  journalist roberto toledo was preparing  to record an interview when he was shot  he died from his wounds in hospital  the director of the online paper toledo  worked for monitor michoacan said the  team had been receiving death threats  for months  today those threats became concrete  exposing corrupt administrations and  corrupt officials and politicians today  led to the death of one of our  colleagues  toledo's killing follows those of three  other mexican journalists in january  2022 alone  one of them was reporter lord maldonado  lopez  she was found shot dead in her car  in the border city of tijuana  in 2019 she had appealed to mexico's  president obrador himself after becoming  involved in a dispute with a prominent  politician and media owner  mr president i've come to ask you for  support help and justice because i fear  for my life  the spate of killings has put reporters  across mexico on edge  and sparked protests asking the  government to step up protection for  media workers  and for more on this now i want to bring  in javier garza he is a journalist in  mexico and a consultant on journalist  protection at the world association of  newspapers javier it's good to have you  on the program i mean the numbers here  are really bad the fourth killing of a  journalist in mexico this year reporters  without borders says 50 journalists have  been killed over the past five years in  mexico how do you explain what's going  on  hi brent thank you for having me uh it's  really tough to explain  because we are looking at two  main main causes um uh or main reasons  why this is  happening one is uh the  the spree of uh attacks against  journalists not just uh  murders murder is of course the most  extreme  form of attack but threats of every kind  spying harassment surveillance et cetera  the spree of ataxicus journalists that  we have been experiencing for the last  15 years  under different governments so this is  not a problem of the current uh federal  government in mexico this is a problem  that we have been accumulating for years  but it has accelerated under this  government because we are seeing that  contrary to what was promised by by  foreign president andres manuel lopez  obrador who uh got to office with with  the  uh campaign platform of eliminating  corruption and eliminating violence  there is a total lack of commitment uh i  would even say a lack of interest  on the part of the government  for tackling this issue and so this  feels impunity  uh this makes uh crime against  journalists to go unpunished and  everybody who thinks about attacking the  journalists uh can can do so with the  knowledge that uh they can get away with  it and reporters without borders labels  mexico the most dangerous place for  journalists in  the world  and based on what you're saying it  sounds like journalists are being  targeted you know not only from the drug  cartels but also possibly from you know  all the way up to the highest levels of  government  yeah journalists are being targeted from  from multiple sources uh usually it is  draw cartels who carry out the most  extreme form of attack which is the  assassination but we have also been  seeing particularly in the last years  this collusion between  criminal organizations drug cartels or  other criminal organizations and local  government officials  at the municipal or the state level  police forces or other security forces  sort of um  conflate  um in order to uh mask the real purpose  of um  killing a journalist let's say for  example a local authority uh has a  problem with the journalists so  an assassination can be ordered but it  can be outsourced so to speak uh to a  criminal group uh and so it will look  like a hit by a criminal organization so  there's all these different combinations  together and sometimes the government  would minimize um the the um the crimes  by saying well the journalist wasn't  really a journalist because he was  working on something else uh the last  case of robert  we saw a response from the federal  government saying that that he was  working on something else not as a  journalist  and so it gets  very murky  and javier we want to remind our viewers  you work as a security consultant for  other journalists and we expect to hear  that when we're talking about war zones  but we're talking about mexico i mean  what safety advice do you give  journalists who are working in mexico  yeah but i i work on on these issues  because i experienced violence uh myself  as the editor of a newspaper that came  under fire from drug cartels in several  locations so some of the lessons that i  learned is that um  each regional dynamic is very different  what's happening in michoacan where we  have the latest killing uh of a  journalist is very different from  tijuana for example where uh the  case of lourdes maldonado for instance  so we have to get to the bottom of the  local circumstances and what are the  dynamics the criminal dynamics the  political dynamics that fuel violence  against journalists but the advice that  i give journalists is you need to have a  safety protocol a safety plan based on  those local dynamics you really have to  know your turf  and design the appropriate safety  measures and  and unfortunately this is true you  cannot you have to be very uh certain  very aware that you cannot rely on the  government for help because they are not  helping on the contrary sometimes  they're making the problems worse yeah  that is a a tragic commentary on the  security situation also on the state of  government by the people in mexico  javier gartha journalist there in mexico  we appreciate your time and your  insights and stay safe thank you  thank you brent
 

